# Python Port Plan — Step 07: The `Boukensha.run` DSL

## Goal

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/07_the_run_dsl` to
`week1_baseline/python/07_the_run_dsl`. This step adds one top-level entry
point, `Boukensha.run(task: "...") do ... end`, that hides all the manual
`Context`/`Registry`/`Backend`/`PromptBuilder`/`Client`/`Logger`/`Agent`
wiring every prior step required — the caller just describes the task and
(optionally) registers tools inline. Ruby achieves the inline block via
`RunDSL#instance_eval`; Python has no equivalent, so per the confirmed
design decision below, the Python `run()` takes an optional `setup`
callback that receives a `RunDSL` object to register tools on before the
run executes.

Also: `mud_host`/`mud_port`/`mud_username`/`mud_password` (removed from
`Config` in step 6) and `LoopError` (removed from `errors.rb` in step 6) are
both **back** in this step's Ruby source, unchanged from their original
(pre-step-6) form. `Logger` gains two more dormant additions: `turn(n:)` and
a `subscribe(&block)` pub/sub hook — neither is called anywhere in this
step's code or README, same pattern as previously dormant features.

**⚠️ Same safety profile as steps 5–6.** `example.py` still runs a real
multi-turn agent loop against the live Anthropic API and writes a real
session JSONL file to the shared `.boukensha/sessions/`.

