## Design decisions (confirmed with user)

- **`run()`'s inline tool-registration mechanism.** Ruby's
  `RunDSL.new(registry).instance_eval(&block) if block` has no Python
  equivalent (no block/instance_eval). Confirmed: Python's `run()` takes an
  optional `setup: Callable[[RunDSL], None] | None = None` keyword-only
  parameter. `run()` builds the `Context`/`Registry` internally (matching
  Ruby's control flow exactly), then — if `setup` was given — calls
  `setup(RunDSL(registry))` to let the caller register tools before the
  backend/agent get built. `RunDSL.tool(...)` reuses the same
  `name, *, description, parameters=None, block=None` signature already
  established for `Registry.tool` in step 2. Usage:

  ```python
  def register_tools(dsl):
      dsl.tool(
          "read_file",
          description="Read the contents of a file from disk",
          parameters={"path": {"type": "string", "description": "The file path to read"}},
          block=lambda *, path: (base_dir / path).read_text(),
      )

  result = boukensha.run(
      task="Read the README.md file and summarise what this MUD player assistant framework can do.",
      setup=register_tools,
  )
  ```

  This was chosen over passing a raw list of tool-spec dicts because it
  preserves the 1:1 method-name correspondence to `RunDSL#tool` and reuses
  the already-solved `block=` kwarg pattern instead of inventing a second,
  data-only tool-registration shape.

## `run()` translation (`lib/boukensha/run_dsl.py`)

Both `RunDSL` and `run()` live in one new file, `run_dsl.py` (mirroring
Ruby's `run_dsl.rb` filename for the DSL host object; Ruby's `run`
classmethod itself lives inline in `boukensha.rb`, but Python keeps
`__init__.py` a thin re-export layer — same principle already applied for
`_state.py` — so the actual `run()` implementation lives in `run_dsl.py`
too, re-exported as `boukensha.run(...)`).

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `cfg = config` (module singleton) | `cfg = config()` (from `._state`) |
| `task_class = Tasks::Player` | `task_class = Player` |
| `task_settings = cfg.tasks(task_class.task_name)` | `task_settings = cfg.tasks(task_class.task_name())` |
| `system \|\|= task_class.system_prompt(...)` | `if system is None: system = task_class.system_prompt(...)` |
| `model \|\|= task_class.model(task_settings)` | `if model is None: model = task_class.model(task_settings)` |
| `backend \|\|= task_class.provider(task_settings).to_sym` | `if backend is None: backend = task_class.provider(task_settings)` (plain string, no symbol conversion) |
| `api_key \|\|= case backend; when :anthropic then ENV["ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"]; ...` | `if api_key is None: api_key = {"anthropic": ..., "openai": ..., "gemini": ..., "ollama_cloud": ...}.get(backend)` reading from `os.environ.get(...)` — no case for `"ollama"` (matches Ruby: `api_key` stays `None`, which is correct since `Ollama` takes no key) |
| `ctx = Context.new(...)`, `registry = Registry.new(ctx)` | Same, unconditionally built before the `setup` callback runs |
| `RunDSL.new(registry).instance_eval(&block) if block` | `if setup is not None: setup(RunDSL(registry))` |
| `be = case backend; when :anthropic then Backends::Anthropic.new(...); ...; else raise ArgumentError` | `if/elif` chain over the same 5 string values, `else: raise ValueError(f"Unknown backend {backend!r}. Use 'anthropic', 'openai', 'gemini', 'ollama', or 'ollama_cloud'.")` (note: Ruby's `backend.inspect` on a symbol renders `:foo`; Python's `{backend!r}` on a string renders `'foo'` — cosmetic deviation, same family as prior string-vs-symbol notes) |
| `builder`, `client` | Same |
| `effective_max_iterations = task_class.max_iterations(task_settings)` | Same |
| `effective_max_output_tokens = max_output_tokens \|\| task_class.max_output_tokens(task_settings)` | `max_output_tokens or task_class.max_output_tokens(task_settings)` |
| `logger = Logger.new(log: log, snapshot: {task:, max_iterations:, max_output_tokens:, model:, provider:})` | `Logger(log=log, snapshot={"task": task_class.task_name(), "max_iterations": ..., "max_output_tokens": ..., "model": model, "provider": backend})` |
| `agent = Agent.new(...)` | Same, passing the same `task_settings`/`max_iterations`/`max_output_tokens` |
| `ctx.add_message(:user, task)` then `agent.run` | `ctx.add_message("user", task)` then `return agent.run()` |
| `ensure logger&.close` | Wrap the **entire function body** in `try/finally`, with `logger = None` declared before the `try` — mirrors Ruby's method-level `ensure` running even if an earlier step (e.g. an invalid `backend` string) raised before `logger` was ever assigned. `finally: if logger is not None: logger.close()` |

